Tensor flow 2.0

tensors are multi dimensional arrays and very similar to numpy arrays.

A neural network can be represented as a graph made up of -
  tensors
  Computation nodes called neurons
  
They are directed acyclic graphs.

In tensorflow 2.0, you can use Staic graphs (lazy execution) as well as Dynamic graphs (eager execution)
You can convert the code written in eager execution mode into a Static graph by using tf.function decorator on your function.

A neural network makes two passes on training data - forward pass and backward pass
In forward pass, the network generates a prediction y^ for all data points
After that it calculates the mean squared loss and applies gradient descent algorithm to generate gradient by which the existing parameters must be updated to reduce the loss.
In the backward pass, the NN uses the value calculated by GD to update the parameters such the overall loss is reduced.

GD in tensorflow 2.0 is calulated by a method called Auto Differentiation and uses the function tf.GradientTape()





